  "solution": {
    "notes": [
      "This is the greedy solution for the stated strict-follow relation a < b < c < d."
    ],
    "approach": "Sort all pairs by their right endpoint. Scan from left to right and select a pair whenever its left endpoint is strictly greater than the right endpoint of the last selected pair. Choosing the earliest finishing compatible pair leaves the most room for the remainder of the chain.",
    "editorial": {
      "better": {
        "complexity": "O(n^2) time and O(n) space.",
        "description": "Use dynamic programming after sorting pairs by a fixed endpoint. Let dp[i] be the longest chain ending at pair i; for every earlier pair j with pairs[j][1] < pairs[i][0], update dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)."
      },
      "optimal": {
        "complexity": "O(n log n) time and O(1) auxiliary space apart from the in-place sorting implementation.",
        "description": "Sort by increasing right endpoint and greedily take the first pair whose left endpoint is greater than the right endpoint of the previously chosen pair. The earliest finishing compatible pair is always safe because any later-finishing compatible choice cannot leave more room for future pairs."
      },
      "bruteForce": {
        "complexity": "O(2^n · n) time in a straightforward subset-based formulation; exponential space or recursion depth depending on implementation.",
        "description": "Try every subset/order of pairs and keep the longest valid chain. This directly follows the definition but has exponential time in the number of pairs."
      }
    },
    "timeComplexity": "O(n log n)",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(1) auxiliary space when sorting in place; O(log n) stack space may be used by the language's sorting implementation.",
    "code": {
      "C": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <ctype.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nstruct Pair { int left, right; };\n\nstatic int cmpPair(const void* a, const void* b) {\n    const struct Pair* x = (const struct Pair*)a;\n    const struct Pair* y = (const struct Pair*)b;\n    return (x->right > y->right) - (x->right < y->right);\n}\n\nint findLongestChain(struct Pair* pairs, int n) {\n    qsort(pairs, n, sizeof(struct Pair), cmpPair);\n    int last = -2147483647, ans = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        if (pairs[i].left > last) {\n            ++ans;\n            last = pairs[i].right;\n        }\n    }\n    return ans;\n}",
      "C++": "class Solution {\npublic:\n    int findLongestChain(vector<vector<int>>& pairs) {\n        sort(pairs.begin(), pairs.end(), [](const auto& a, const auto& b) {\n            return a[1] < b[1];\n        });\n        int last = INT_MIN, ans = 0;\n        for (const auto& p : pairs) {\n            if (p[0] > last) {\n                ++ans;\n                last = p[1];\n            }\n        }\n        return ans;\n    }\n};",
      "Java": "class Solution {\n    public int findLongestChain(int[][] pairs) {\n        Arrays.sort(pairs, Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a[1]));\n        int last = Integer.MIN_VALUE;\n        int ans = 0;\n        for (int[] p : pairs) {\n            if (p[0] > last) {\n                ans++;\n                last = p[1];\n            }\n        }\n        return ans;\n    }\n}",
      "Python3": "import sys, json\n\nclass Solution:\n    def findLongestChain(self, pairs):\n        pairs.sort(key=lambda p: p[1])\n        last = -10**30\n        ans = 0\n        for left, right in pairs:\n            if left > last:\n                ans += 1\n                last = right\n        return ans",
      "JavaScript": "'use strict';\nconst fs = require('fs');\n\nfunction findLongestChain(pairs) {\n    pairs.sort((a, b) => a[1] - b[1]);\n    let last = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY, ans = 0;\n    for (const [left, right] of pairs) {\n        if (left > last) {\n            ans++;\n            last = right;\n        }\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n\nconst args = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(0, 'utf8').trim());\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(findLongestChain(args[0])));"
    }
  },
